    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2764.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 724,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to understand water truly is to understand that it is the absolute foundation of life as we know it. Here is a breakdown of what water is through different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Physical Substance\nOn Earth, water is unique because it is the only natural substance that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** What we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible moisture in the air, clouds, and steam from boiling pots.\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has strange and remarkable properties that defy normal chemical behavior, making it \"the universal solvent\":\n*   **It dissolves almost anything:** Because of its molecular structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it carries nutrients through our bodies and minerals through the earth.\n*   **It expands when it freezes:** Almost all liquids shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all marine life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why the oceans help regulate the climate of the entire planet, and why our bodies don't overheat instantly.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules love to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n*   **The Catch:** Of all that water, **96.5% is salt water** (in oceans), which humans and land animals cannot drink. About **1.7% is locked in glaciers and ice caps**, and another **1.7% is ground water** or atmospheric vapor. \n*   **Freshwater:** Less than **1%** of all the water on Earth is easily accessible freshwater available for human use (rivers, lakes, and shallow groundwater).\n\n### 4. Biological Necessity\nWater is the medium in which life evolved and the fluid in which all life processes happen:\n*   **Human Biology:** The adult human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are roughly 31% water.\n*   **Survival:** While a human can survive for weeks without food, they can generally only survive **3 to 5 days without water**. It regulates our internal temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and delivers oxygen to our cells.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary powers. It shapes geography, drives the weather, covers most of our planet, and flows through the veins of every living creature."
